d: abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n\nBudd-Chiari Syndrome vs Acute Pancreatitis:\n  Favours first:  acute hepatic failure, ascites, budd-chiari syndrome, cholecystitis, cirrhosis\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant inheritance, chronic pancreatitis, fatty stool, insulin-dependent but ketosis-resistant diabetes, nausea\n\nBudd-Chiari Syndrome vs Hepatic Vascular Disorders:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, acute hepatic failure, ascites, budd-chiari syndrome, cholecystitis\n  Favours second: abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n\nAcute Pancreatitis vs Hepatic Vascular Disorders:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, chronic pancreatitis, fatty stool, fever, insulin-dependent but ketosis-resistant diabetes\n  Favours second: abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n\nRelated to Acute Biliary Tract Disease: bile duct disease, bile reflux, biliary tract neoplasm (disease)\n\nRelated to Budd-Chiari Syndrome: hepatic vascular disease\n\nRelated to Acute Pancreatitis: acute disease, alcohol-related disorders, autoimmune pancreatitis\n\nRelated to Hepatic Vascular Disorders: budd-chiari syndrome, hepatic infarction, hepatic vein thrombosis\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → arthralgia → Acute Biliary Tract Disease\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → abdominal distention → Acute Biliary Tract Disease\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → pain → Hepatic Vascular Disorde